    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This work treats a theoretical investigation of forward combustion in a simulated laboratory combustion tube. For this purpose, a mathematical model has been developed that includes the thermal, thermodynamic, and hydrodynamic aspects of the process. In this model, oil is assumed 23 to burn directly, in a fashion similar to Gottfried's assumption. This 53 model is an improvement over the model of Crookston et al. in that the computer program is designed to handle phase disappearance. In addition, we use a fully implicit scheme in a simultaneous technique which seems to be much faster than the Gottfried model. The model shows a temperature profile that matches the combustion tube runs done at Stanford Petroleum Research Institute (SUPRI). In this respect, the model appears to be 52 superior to the model presented by Verma. Verma's model produces temperature profiles that differ greatly from those produced at SUPRI. Also, Verma's model differs considerably from Gottfried's results.Our model yields spatial distributions of pressure and temperature and saturations and concentrations of all important components (oil, water, oxygen, inert gas, and steam). Finally, it gives production rates, cumulative production, and instantaneous and cumulative WORs and GORs.In this work, we present interpretation of the various computed profiles and the associated phenomena. Comparison with other numerical models is also included.The developed model can also simulate other thermal recovery processes, such as hot water flooding and steam injection. Results for these processes are presented and briefly discussed.Having one model that simulates different thermal recovery processes enables a researcher to compare fairly these processes under the same assumptions and numerical scheme.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
